## 3. Membership billing models

### Model A — Registration fee + recurring membership dues

Suitable for an organization with an initial joining fee and recurring membership dues.

```text id="f8p4cz"
MEMBER REGISTRATION
        |
        v
REGISTRATION FEE
        |
        v
MEMBERSHIP APPROVAL
        |
        v
MEMBERSHIP ACTIVATION
        |
        v
ANNUAL / MONTHLY DUES
        |
        v
PAYMENT COLLECTION
        |
        v
MEMBERSHIP STATUS UPDATE
```

### Model B — Annual membership renewal

Suitable for associations, clubs, and communities with a fixed annual renewal cycle.

```text id="n1n0xb"
ACTIVE MEMBER
      |
      v
RENEWAL PERIOD
      |
      v
MEMBERSHIP DUES
      |
      v
PAYMENT REQUEST
      |
      v
PAYMENT CONFIRMED
      |
      v
RENEWAL PROCESSED
```

The organization defines the membership validity period and renewal rules.

### Model C — Membership tiers

Suitable when members pay different fees based on their category.

Examples:

- Individual.
- Student.
- Professional.
- Corporate.
- Premium.
- Lifetime membership.

Use appropriate Collection and Plan structures where supported.

### Model D — Community contributions

Suitable for periodic or one-time contributions.

Examples:

- Community fund contributions.
- Activity fees.
- Event participation.
- Cooperative contributions.
- Special fundraising obligations.

The organization must distinguish voluntary contributions from contractual or mandatory membership dues.

### Model E — Membership with privileges

Suitable when members receive benefits based on their membership category or payment status.

Examples:

- Member discounts.
- Priority event access.
- Training access.
- Partner offers.
- Community services.

Benefit eligibility should be managed by the organization's membership or entitlement system unless a specific integration has been validated.

